ExistsError` that had replaced it with a bare `return`. Verified differentially against a throwaway worktree of `8a02e17` rather than by reading: the five-shape probe (absent / existing dir / symlink→existing dir / symlink→missing dir / regular file) now agrees with the pre-fix baseline on all five rows.\n\nOne correction to the verdict on a detail: `provision_new_manager_home` itself returned *cleanly* on the regular-file input — the raw `FileExistsError` the reviewer saw comes from a later `mkdir`, once locking went on to use the accepted non-home. Both are fail-open so the fix is unchanged, but the new `GlobalLock` test pins the `LockError`-not-`OSError` boundary `cli.main` depends on, which is the part that mattered.\n\n**2 — the pairwise scan is pinned as gone.** The new test counts every read of the state a namespace is compared by, at three equally spaced sizes: 156 / 296 / 436 units for 32 / 60 / 88 namespaces, against 3,500 / 12,446 / 26,880 for a pairwise scan over the same memoised probes. Red-proved by actually restoring that scan — it fails this test alone, both older cost tests staying green, exactly as predicted.\n\n**3 — curator `LOGBOOK.md`** now carries the quadratic validation as a POSIX-latent cost defect (750,620 canonicalisations, 182s of 210s, on macOS) and the `completedAt` anomaly.\n\n## A platform assumption I had backwards\n\nWith `ssh win` still offline, I reasoned that Windows `CreateDirectoryW` on a dangling symlink would reparse to the destination and create a directory there, and had a platform-tolerant version of that test staged instead of the strict one. The runner settled it: it returns `ERROR_ALREADY_EXISTS`. Both symlink tests **ran** on `windows-latest` rather than skipping — +6 passed and **zero** new skips on every cell — so the strict `LockError`-on-every-platform assertion is correct and is what shipped. No test was weakened for a behavior that had only been reasoned about.\n\n## Evidence — real exit codes, each command standalone\n\n| command | exit | result |\n|---|---|---|\n| `python -m mypy` | 0 | no issues in 67 source files |\n| new locking tests | 0 | 5 passed |\n| same, fix reverted | **1** | 3 failed, 2 passed — red proof |\n| `test_transactions -k namespace` | 0 | 19 passed |\n| same, pairwise restored | **1** | 1 failed, 18 passed — red proof |\n| focused suites | 0 | 162 passed / 4 skipped (156 + 6) |\n| full suite | 0 | **1151 passed / 100 skipped** (1145 + 6) |\n\n**PR 16 run `30641011440` on head `f8b90a5`: 14/14 green.** Windows cells report 1217 passed / 152 skipped each (672 / 851 / 493 / 660 s), read from the job logs — note `gh run view --log` came back empty for this run while `gh api .../jobs/<id>/logs` returned the full 233 KB.\n\nSigned commit `f8b90a5` (`%G? = G`), pushed to `ivanopcode/cocoaskills` only; no tags. The open product decision on repairing a pre-existing Administrators-owned Windows home is unchanged and deliberately not folded in. Curator `LOGBOOK.md` is left uncommitted in the working tree, as prior rounds did.","ttft_ms":2556,"ttft_stream_ms":1979,"time_to_request_ms":81,"type":"result","duration_ms":1971535,"uuid":"99642fec-8b77-4f34-8f90-49271e0627ea"}
